A||||3||fiero87||Lots of ???  Can anyone help?||||5||
Z||000000||fiero87||10-26-2000||01:01 PM||||Hello fellow fourm members,<P>I recently picked up my new 86 GT.  I have owned my 87 coupe for two years now, but this gt is completly different and i have some questions i was hoping somone maybe able to help with...<BR>1.  The throttle cable is 10x stiffer than in my coupe...Is this normal?  Do i need a new cable or TB?  <BR>2.  There is some really bad what i think to be lifter noise coming from the engine at idle and lower RPMS.  Can hardly hear the exhaust over it.  Do i need new lifters, or more headwork done?  <BR>3.  This car seems really sluggish.  I'm used to driving my Turbo Daytona, and v-6 duster...they seem to pull harder than this car..  Something tells me this isn't right.  could the lifter problem be stealing my power?  It probably needs a full tune up as well.  <BR>4.  The idle after warm up is somewhere between 2000, and 15000...I read this could be a bad EGR tube...is this true?  What are the other possobilites.  <BR>5.  Finally i have an oppertunity to buy another v-6 from an 85 with an auto...Can this be easily installed in my 86 with a 4 sp muncie?  What needs to be done?  Apparently this engine has slight rod knock, but was rebuilt 10k ago.  Bad rod bearing?  Is it worth the possibly $300 i could spend?  <P>I know this is a lot of questions, but if anyone could help with any of these i would be extremely appreciative.  Thank you <P>Tim||||reg||5
Z||000001||ThatRickGuy||10-26-2000||01:15 PM||||1. easy check. well, mostly. pull off the throttle, kick down and cruise cables from the TB. try flipping it by hand. if it's still hard,pull off the intake tube and look at the inside of the TB. I've heard of them getting gummed up with carbon, I know there was a decent ammount in mine when I tore it down, but not enough to make it stick.<P>2.Bad lifter noise... well, nothing a cam, new lifters and new rockers won't fix. if you're looking for a cheap temp fix, you could try to just retighten the rockers, but if everything is worn out and beat up, replacement it your best bet.<P>3. Fieros need tune ups! maintainence is one of the most important things. new spark plugs, wired, coil, dist cap, rotor, filters, etc. You can get all the parts you need and do it with average tools for under $100 in a day.<P>4. Tune up is cheaper then a new EGR. I'd say try that first, then try to trouble shoot the high idle. Just by chance, my engine was idling high 1500-2000 when I first started it up after the rebuild. The ECM takes like 20 miles to reset itself.<P>5. the 2 should be an exact match except the flex plate/fly wheel, just grab it with the other engine. But rod knock is bad. I only head 1 rod knocking when I pulled my engine out. all 6 of the rods were wasted. $300 isn't much for an engine, but throw on a few extra hundred to make that engine reliable.||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||10-26-2000||01:24 PM||||1) Not normal.  Try cleaning throttle body and linkage first.<P>2) Could be lifters; don't know.  Obviously, if it's lifters, rate of tapping will be proportional to engine RPMs.  You probably knew that already.  Could also be exhaust leak.<P>3) Think you answered your own question, here. <P>4) Probably vacuum leak of some kind (e.g. - EGR tube, etc.).  Could be PCV fitting came out of valve cover - that could explain item  2, if you're lucky  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>5) Think it will work; don't know what value of the new(er) engine should be.  Obviously, need to swap flywheel for flex plate, etc.<P> <BR>   ||||reg||1
Z||000003||william_thorniii||10-26-2000||01:42 PM||||Fiero87<P>Congrats on the recent addition to the family :~)  I will get flamed for this, buy that 86 will never be as reliable as your DIS Duke.  I have 2 V-6 cars and my 87 Coupe and I would NEVER get rid of my coupe.  <P>1) The cable could actually just be bound up as well.  Fiero56 has a e-brake cable (not the same, but close) that was once bound but now is free.  He pulled it off, would hit the ends with a healthy amount of lube and work the cable until it eventually freed up.  Long shot but possible.  <P>2) Try listening with a hose to determine area of engine where sound is originating.  If the hose trick doesn't work, try feeling for the vibration with a screw driver.  <P>3) As much as most of us hate to admit it... a perfectly running Fiero will be sluggish in comparison to a Chrysler Turbo 3.0L... hmm possible swap candidate? <P>4) I want that engine if it idles at 15,000 RPM.  That is close to the limiter on a CHAMP car.  I agree with the others here... just give her a tune-up first then look for that fatal EGR tube crack.  The most prolific cause of a problem like this would be some sort of vacume leak though.  Try spraying all areas of engine bay with water while running... if the idle changes significantly you have a vacume leak.<P>5)  I wouldn't buy any engine that someone admits to having rebuilt unless I knew them and knew their quality of work.  A rod knock can be an easy thing to fix, or it can be a difficult thing to fix.  If you do it right, you must bore & hone the cyl, replace the rod, bearings, remachine the crank, yada, yada, yada...  Then again the clown might have just gone in and put a plastic shim in there.  <P>William T. Thorn, III   <BR>||||reg||3
